a senior manager at amazon who is suing  the e-commerce giant for racial  discrimination  is speaking exclusively to cbs this  morning in her first television  interview  charlotte newman filed a federal lawsuit  against amazon and  two managers claiming she was underpaid  stereotyped  sexually harassed and sexually assaulted  newman alleges the discrimination began  during her hiring process in 2017  when she says she was offered a job  below the level  and pay she had applied for it's a  practice known as down leveling  cbs this morning saturday co-host  michelle miller sat down with the amazon  web services  senior manager there's a problem with  the culture and with practices  charlotte newman says she's speaking out  now because she thinks it's time for a  change in amazon  it's time for amazon to act with the  same obsession internally with the same  rigor that we approach  our businesses and our customers in the  lawsuit  newman who's still employed by amazon  alleges the company pays her less  than people with similar experience she  says she's also been the victim of  stereotypical  racial tropes in one instance she says a  co-worker told her  she looked like a gorilla and she says a  manager called her aggressive  too direct and just scary i'm not here  because  it's just about me as the vox reporting  demonstrates  there's a pattern of systemic  discrimination newman's lawsuit came  shortly  after vox's website recode published an  investigation  into treatment of black employees at  amazon  i think a lot of these issues have been  festering at the company  senior recode correspondent jason del  rey says  he spoke to 10 black employees who told  him  they face both direct and insidious bias  that harms their careers and personal  lives  there are some really awful experiences  relate to me  one comes to mind you know about a a  black employee who had  a manager from another team tell her  just sort of  unprompted that he's pretty sure his  ancestors had slaves at some point but  but he thinks that  they were treated well recode also  reported that internal amazon data shows  black corporate employees are promoted  less frequently  and rated more harshly by managers than  non-black  employees in response an amazon  spokesperson told us  we work hard to make amazon a company  where our black employees and people of  all backgrounds feel included  respected and want to grow their careers  and they do not tolerate discrimination  or harassment in any form  amazon needs to do more than just make  statements about black lives matter  and make statements about what they're  doing for black employees and other  employees of color  and have that actually show up in how  employees are being treated  newman says for her it went beyond  racial discrimination and  unfair pay in her lawsuit she says  management engaged in repeated sexual  harassment  including incidents of inappropriate  unwelcome touching and sexual assault  she says she was groped by a manager at  a work function  he put his hand on my thigh and began to  like rub and kind of caress my thigh  and move up my leg and  i froze and was  just completely embarrassed and just  stunned at like such a brazen act  you know all of the time spent ensuring  that  you know i'm coming into work and being  a professional  just to be sort of reduced to to that  was very difficult  the alleged incident happened in 2018  but newman says she did not report it  until last  year i think he understood the gravity  of what had happened in some ways  but i also think because he chose to put  his hands on me again  clearly you know he like to me he  demonstrated that he's a predator  what happened the second time i was  again at a work  like with colleagues this was at an off  site and i was getting ready to leave  and he grabbed my braids and  he made like an offhand comment about  like you can leave these here  why did you decide to stay at amazon  well i think all too often  the brunt of sexual harassment and  discrimination it falls on the victim  and i felt why should i be the one  to leave one of the biggest fastest  growing companies in the world  an amazon spokesperson told cbs news we  do not tolerate discrimination or  harassment of any kind  we immediately investigated ms newman's  sexual harassment claim  and fired her harasser they added we  also reviewed miss newman's  interview process leveling and  onboarding  and determined that she was properly  placed in her role at the company  can things get better at amazon for you  i hope they get better for all employees  as i was coming here today i was  thinking about a quote by coretta scott  king  it essentially says that struggle never  ends you  constantly fight for freedom and that  every generation essentially has to  do that that was our colleague michelle  miller reporting we do have a bit more  from amazon a spokesperson tells cbs  news the investigation into newman's  alleged sexual harassment resulted in  additional training requirements for  those in her reporting line  they are also investigating the new  allegations in her lawsuit they say  and they said they've set a number of  targets company-wide  commitments to diversity telling us  they've doubled representation of black  directors and vice presidents  last year and they've committed to do  the same in 2021  well let's hope they want to do the work  i say bravo to ms newman a for raising  the issues and b  for staying yeah we're staying because  that's how you're going to have change  you have to stay you have to speak up  and people have to hear you and she said  why should i be the one to leave exactly  exactly right it was very difficult  though to hear somebody at the company  say  you know my family had slaves or  whatever he said but they were treated  well i lost a little bit of hearing  after that  such an oxymoron
 

